#: ../../../reference/interfaces/browser-support-interface.md:2
msgid "browser-support interface"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/browser-support-interface.md:4
msgid ""
"`browser-support` allows access to various APIs needed by modern web "
"browsers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/browser-support-interface.md:6
msgid ""
"**Auto-connect**: no when `allow-sandbox: true`, yes otherwise "
"**Attributes**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/browser-support-interface.md:8
msgid "`allow-sandbox: true|false` (defaults to ``false``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/browser-support-interface.md:10
msgid ""
"This interface is intended to be used when using an embedded Chromium "
"Content API or using the sandboxes in major browsers from vendors like "
"Google and Mozilla. The ``allow-sandbox`` attribute may be used to give the "
"necessary access to use the browser's sandbox functionality."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/browser-support-interface.md:12
msgid ""
"ⓘ A browser's internal sandbox requires numerous privileged security policy "
"rules to work and is typically considered trusted outside of *snapd*. For "
"this reason, `--allow-sandbox=true` is limited to trusted publishers only."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/browser-support-interface.md:14
msgid ""
"For some web applications, such those using Electron, it is often useful to "
"disable the internal sandbox and rely on strict confinement, forcing the "
"snap to use `allow-sandbox: false`. To do this, specify `--no-sandbox` on "
"the command line for your application."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/interfaces/browser-support-interface.md:16
msgid ""
"When `--no-sandbox` is used, the snap is confined regardless of the `--allow-"
"sandbox` attribute, which now only controls whether or not the browser's "
"internal sandbox can be used."
msgstr ""
